{-
   A `bitcast` that changes the lane *count* - Design-Vector §3.4's third rule, and the one case of it
   that has no JavaScript form.

   `Widen` and `Narrow` keep the lane count and change the lane type; `Bitcast` keeps the byte width
   and is otherwise free. So `i32x4` to `i16x8` is a rung and `i32x4` to `i16x4` is not, which is the
   whole of the safety argument: no instance relating two vectors of different byte widths was ever
   generated, so nothing downstream has to ask whether a reinterpretation fits.

   Its own fixture rather than a half of `VecWidth.yana` because this is exactly what a lane cannot be
   on JS: sixteen bytes read as four numbers cannot be read as eight, since a lane there is a number
   rather than a run of bits, and the backend says so. `VecWidth.yana` asserts that the two targets
   agree; this asserts the case where one of them declines, and so has no `.js.expect`.
-}

let &sink = 0 :: Vec(I32, 4)

@noinline
fn regrouping(v: Vec(I32, 4)) -> Vec(I16, 8):
    return bitcast(v)

@noinline
fn bytes(v: Vec(I16, 8)) -> Vec(U8, 16):
    return bitcast(v)

@noinline
fn back(v: Vec(U8, 16)) -> Vec(I32, 4):
    return bitcast(v)

fn main() -> Int:
    -- Sixteen bytes read three ways and put back, which has to be the value it started as: every step
    -- is the same register under another name.
    sink = back(bytes(regrouping(sink)))
    return 1
